Rem ==================================================================
Rem julian.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: the calendar as arithmetic
Rem
Rem The one place the civil calendar is spelled. Before this machine
Rem the Gregorian leap rules shipped twice — fin.shoddy carried a
Rem serial-day arithmetic for its bond words and geo.shoddy carried a
Rem Julian day number for its sun words — and fin's own header called
Rem its copy a stand-in. Both now define their words over this machine,
Rem so there is one set of rules and not two.
Rem
Rem PURE THROUGHOUT, AND IT NEVER READS A CLOCK. A date arrives as an
Rem argument or it does not arrive. clock.shoddy is the capability that
Rem knows what today is, and ClockDate there is the one bridge — built
Rem that way so that fin, geo and ephemeris stay out of the capability
Rem system entirely, exactly as geo's header promises.
Rem
Rem Conventions, all of them load-bearing:
Rem
Rem   * THE CALENDAR IS THE PROLEPTIC GREGORIAN. The leap rules run
Rem     unchanged back past 1582 and into negative years, because a
Rem     calendar that switches rulebooks mid-history would make every
Rem     serial ambiguous. Dates in genuinely Julian-calendar documents
Rem     need converting by the caller, who knows the document.
Rem
Rem   * TWO EPOCHS, ONE SET OF RULES, ONE CONSTANT. JulSerial counts
Rem     whole days from 1970-01-01 (serial 0), the epoch finance and
Rem     computing already use. JulDayNumber is the astronomical Julian
Rem     day at 00:00 UT — noon-based, so a civil midnight always ends
Rem     in .5 — and it is DEFINED OVER JulSerial, so 2440587.5 appears
Rem     in exactly one place. A machine per epoch would spell the leap
Rem     rules twice, which is the disease this machine cures.
Rem
Rem   * A FRACTIONAL SERIAL IS REFUSED, AND THE GUARD IS LOAD-BEARING.
Rem     The commonest way to confuse the two epochs is to hand a Julian
Rem     day number to the serial words — and a Julian day number ends
Rem     in .5. JulFromSerial aborts on a fraction instead of answering
Rem     a plausible date some five thousand years wide of the mark.
Rem
Rem   * FIELDS CARRY THE PREFIX: JulYear, JulMonth, JulDay. An accessor
Rem     loses every naming contest, and Year, Month and Day are words
Rem     every program wants as locals. fin's retired date record spelt
Rem     them Yr, Mon and Day for the same reason and still had to warn
Rem     about it; the prefix removes the contest instead of narrowing
Rem     it.
Rem
Rem   * = ON TWO DATES IS HONEST, unlike geo's points and eng's complex
Rem     numbers. A JulDate is whole-number arithmetic end to end, so a
Rem     computed date lands exactly on its literal or the arithmetic is
Rem     wrong. There is no JulNear and none is missing.
Rem
Rem   * YEARS RUN -400000..400000, WHOLE. The bound is far inside 2^53
Rem     for every arithmetic here; it exists so that an infinity or a
Rem     NaN fails a positive range test rather than flowing into the
Rem     era arithmetic (a comparison against NaN is False, so both are
Rem     refused by the same check — the trick geo's builders use).
Rem
Rem   * WEEKDAYS ARE 1..7, MONDAY IS 1. The ISO convention, 1-based as
Rem     everything in Shoddy is. Serial 0 was a Thursday, which is the
Rem     whole derivation of the formula below.
Rem
Rem   * NO PARSING, NO FORMATTING, NO TIME. Text is str's business and
Rem     a caller's format decision; hours are a Number argument in the
Rem     machines that need one (geo's sun words, ephemeris); political
Rem     time zones are a database no machine should ship, and the
Rem     nautical zone is already geo's GeoZoneOffset. This machine is
Rem     the calendar's arithmetic, not its politics.
Rem
Rem Includes nothing, deliberately — a tier-one machine like math, so
Rem any program can pull the calendar in alone.
Rem ==================================================================

Rem ---- the builtins this machine is built on -------------------------
Rem
Rem NOT Defs. The engine dispatches these; they are listed because a
Rem reader checking the era arithmetic should not have to leave the
Rem file to find out what they do.

Rem Fix(x As Number) As Number                        ( x -- y )
Rem     Truncation TOWARD ZERO, where Floor goes toward minus infinity.
Rem     The era arithmetic below leans on that difference: it shifts
Rem     negative years by 399 first precisely so that Fix lands the era
Rem     where Floor would.

Rem Wrap(a As Number, b As Number) As Number          ( a b -- r )
Rem     FLOORED modulo: the answer takes its sign from b, so a negative
Rem     serial still yields a weekday in 1..7.

Rem Ifte(c, t, f)                                     ( c t f -- x )
Rem     The inline conditional, for the one-line branches below where
Rem     an indented If would triple a Def's height.

Rem ---- the type --------------------------------------------------------

Rem Year, month, day, in the order a date is written. Both the fields
Rem and their order are load-bearing: fields are POSITIONAL, so
Rem inserting one here would shift every later constructor argument in
Rem the tree silently.
Type JulDate
    JulYear  As Number
    JulMonth As Number
    JulDay   As Number

Rem ---- the leap rules, spelled once --------------------------------

Def JulIsLeap(y As Number) As Boolean
    (y Mod 4 = 0 And y Mod 100 <> 0) Or y Mod 400 = 0

Def JulDaysInMonth(y As Number, m As Number) As Number
    If m = 2 Then
        Ifte(JulIsLeap(y), 29, 28)
    Else
        Ifte(m = 4 Or m = 6 Or m = 9 Or m = 11, 30, 31)

Rem ---- construction ----------------------------------------------------

Rem One set of rules for both builders, so there are not two. THE WORD
Rem NAME IS AN ARGUMENT because JulOf must abort naming JULOF and
Rem JulTryOf must refuse naming JULTRYOF — an error naming the other
Rem one is an error a caller cannot act on. The tests are written
Rem POSITIVELY so that NaN and infinity fail them for free.
Def JulChkOf(word As String, y As Number, m As Number, d As Number) As Result
    If Not (y >= -400000 And y <= 400000 And Floor(y) = y) Then
        Err(word & ": YEAR '" & Str(y) & "' IS NOT A WHOLE NUMBER IN -400000..400000", 0)
    Else
        If Not (m >= 1 And m <= 12 And Floor(m) = m) Then
            Err(word & ": MONTH '" & Str(m) & "' IS NOT A WHOLE NUMBER IN 1..12", 0)
        Else
            If Not (d >= 1 And d <= JulDaysInMonth(y, m) And Floor(d) = d) Then
                Err(word & ": DAY '" & Str(d) & "' DOES NOT FALL IN THAT MONTH", 0)
            Else
                Ok(JulDate(y, m, d))

Rem The total twin, for a date that came from outside. At is 0
Rem throughout: the offending value is a number that was handed over,
Rem not a character in a string that was read.
Def JulTryOf(y As Number, m As Number, d As Number) As Result
    JulChkOf("JULTRYOF", y, m, d)

Rem The checked builder, DEFINED OVER THE TOTAL TWIN — the pairing
Rem bool, str and geo all use. A bad date from inside the program is a
Rem bug in the program and aborts.
Def JulOf(y As Number, m As Number, d As Number) As JulDate
    Select Case JulChkOf("JULOF", y, m, d)
        Case Ok(dd)
            dd
        Case Err(why, at)
            Error(why)
        Case Else
            Error("JULOF: THE DOMAIN CHECK DID NOT ANSWER")

Rem ---- the two epochs --------------------------------------------------

Rem Days from civil: shift the year to start in March so the leap day
Rem lands last, then it is all integer arithmetic and the century rules
Rem take care of themselves. This is the arithmetic fin.shoddy shipped
Rem and proved; it moved here unchanged, and tst/julian.shoddy holds it
Rem to fin's own fixtures.
Def JulSerial(d As JulDate) As Number
    Let y = Ifte(JulMonth(d) <= 2, JulYear(d) - 1, JulYear(d))
    Let era = Fix(Ifte(y >= 0, y, y - 399) / 400)
    Let yoe = y - era * 400
    Let mp = (JulMonth(d) + 9) Mod 12
    Let doy = Fix((153 * mp + 2) / 5) + JulDay(d) - 1
    Let doe = yoe * 365 + Fix(yoe / 4) - Fix(yoe / 100) + doy
    era * 146097 + doe - 719468

Rem Civil from days. THE GUARD IS THE POINT (see the header): a serial
Rem is a whole count of days, and the likeliest fraction to arrive here
Rem is a Julian day number's .5, which without the guard would answer a
Rem plausible date five thousand years wide of the mark.
Def JulFromSerial(n As Number) As JulDate
    If Floor(n) <> n Then
        Error("JULFROMSERIAL: '" & Str(n) & "' IS NOT A WHOLE COUNT OF DAYS - A .5 IS USUALLY A JULIAN DAY NUMBER ON THE WRONG EPOCH")
    Else
        JulFromWhole(n)

Def JulFromWhole(n As Number) As JulDate
    Let z = n + 719468
    Let era = Fix(Ifte(z >= 0, z, z - 146096) / 146097)
    Let doe = z - era * 146097
    Let yoe = Fix((doe - Fix(doe / 1460) + Fix(doe / 36524) - Fix(doe / 146096)) / 365)
    Let y = yoe + era * 400
    Let doy = doe - (365 * yoe + Fix(yoe / 4) - Fix(yoe / 100))
    Let mp = Fix((5 * doy + 2) / 153)
    Let d = doy - Fix((153 * mp + 2) / 5) + 1
    Let m = Ifte(mp < 10, mp + 3, mp - 9)
    JulDate(Ifte(m <= 2, y + 1, y), m, d)

Rem The astronomical Julian day number at 00:00 UT — noon-based, so a
Rem civil midnight always ends in .5. DEFINED OVER JulSerial: this line
Rem is the only place the offset between the epochs exists, and it is
Rem the relation tst/julian.shoddy asserts verbatim.
Def JulDayNumber(d As JulDate) As Number
    JulSerial(d) + 2440587.5

Rem ---- arithmetic ------------------------------------------------------

Rem Signed, b minus a — the order every "days until" question wants,
Rem and the order fin's actual-basis day count already used.
Def JulDiff(a As JulDate, b As JulDate) As Number
    JulSerial(b) - JulSerial(a)

Def JulAddDays(d As JulDate, days As Number) As JulDate
    JulFromSerial(JulSerial(d) + days)

Rem Clamped to month end: one month after 31 January is 28 February,
Rem not the 3rd of March. The coupon-schedule word, which is why a
Rem negative count must walk cleanly backwards.
Def JulAddMonths(d As JulDate, months As Number) As JulDate
    Let total = JulYear(d) * 12 + (JulMonth(d) - 1) + months
    Let yy = Fix(total / 12)
    Let mm = total - yy * 12 + 1
    JulDate(yy, mm, Min(JulDay(d), JulDaysInMonth(yy, mm)))

Rem 1..7, Monday 1 — the ISO convention. Serial 0 was a Thursday, so
Rem shifting by 3 puts Monday at the bottom of the wrap; Wrap rather
Rem than Mod so a date before 1970 still answers 1..7.
Def JulWeekday(d As JulDate) As Number
    Wrap(JulSerial(d) + 3, 7) + 1
